{\pard\sa160 A real record has findings of its own before anything is planted: this one has C10's malformed pointer, found twice. The control measures them, and each defect is judged against that baseline: found as expected, and nothing beyond what the record already had. Result: {\f1 defects-questions-before-priors.md}, all seven found.\par}
{\pard\sa160 This runs the deterministic checks only. The Stage-2 evaluation adds the model-judged checks, more papers and both modalities, blind runs on more than one model, repeatability against the published tolerance, and the two guardrail cases (I.4).\par}
{\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b Recall of prior work (appendix I.4)\par}
{\pard\sa160 {\f1 mark1 recall <paper> <run-id> --expect DOI [--expect DOI ...]} reads a run's {\f1 sieve/candidates.json} and evidence map and writes {\f1 recall-<paper>.md}: for each prior work a reader knows the map should reach, whether retrieval reached it and by which query or citation count, and what the map made of it. It is reported as it happened; nothing is tuned to the answer.\par}
{\pard\sa160 For {\i Questions Before Priors} the expected works are the seven on awareness the paper itself cites in venues the map searches: Karni and Vier\u248? (2013, 2017), Heifetz, Meier and Schipper (2006), Schipper (2021), Vier\u248? (2021), Dekel, Lipman and Rustichini (1998), and Fudenberg and Levine (1993). Result on the live journal run ({\f1 recall-questions-before-priors.md}): {\b 3 of 7}, all three through the snowball, none from the keyword queries. The four it missed are in journals it searched ({\i JET}, {\i Econometrica}); the paper's own references seed the snowball's counts but never become candidates themselves, and that is the fix the result points to. (The synthetic record's recall test, 2 of 2 on 2026-10-21, was removed with that record.)\par}
